"Both radicals are positive. Squaring their sum gives 18+2√[(9+2√5)(9−2√5)]=18+2√(81−20)=18+2√61. Take the positive square root to obtain √(18+2√61).", "topic": "Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities", "note": "Original PDF visually checked; isolated complete question and independently verified the solution.", "source_document": "Math_Question_Bank_Ghoneem_Clean.pdf", "source_page": 119, "extra_sources": []}, {"id": "clean:HOA 203", "old_status": "ocr_screened_not_verified", "status": "ready", "question_id": "38cf56db-a9cd-535f-9f37-78871c9aa8fd", "stem": "What is the product of the roots of −2x²+2x+3=0?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "−1.5"}, {"key": "B", "text": "1.5"}, {"key": "C", "text": "1"}, {"key": "D", "text": "−1"}], "type": "mcq", "correct": "A", "explanation": "For ax²+bx+c=0, the product of roots is c/a. Here it is 3/(−2)=−1.5.", "topic": "Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities", "note": "Restored the missing =0. The preceding pie chart lacks the orange label, so its probability question remains excluded.", "source_document": "Math_Question_Bank_Ghoneem_Clean.pdf", "source_page": 120, "extra_sources": []}, {"id": "clean:HOA 204", "old_status": "ocr_screened_not_verified", "status": "ready", "question_id": "f16531c7-e18a-5022-8c41-28fcb12f4c1f", "stem": "If √(x−2)−a=5 and a=−3, what is x?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "4"}, {"key": "B", "text": "6"}, {"key": "C", "text": "14"}, {"key": "D", "text": "19"}], "type": "mcq", "correct": "B", "explanation": "Substitute a=−3: √(x−2)+3=5. Hence √(x−2)=2, so x−2=4 and x=6. This satisfies the original equation.", "topic": "Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities", "note": "Original PDF visually checked; isolated complete question and independently verified the solution.", "source_document": "Math_Question_Bank_Ghoneem_Clean.pdf", "source_page": 121, "extra_sources": []}, {"id": "clean:HOA 205", "old_status": "ocr_screened_not_verified", "status": "ready", "question_id": "60fe3b58-6183-5360-bdc9-4a65409daf26", "stem": "If 243^(x+1)=27^(2x−3), what is x?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "14"}, {"key": "B", "text": "4"}, {"key": "C", "text": "19"}, {"key": "D", "text": "16"}], "type": "mcq", "correct": "A", "explanation": "Use base 3: 5(x+1)=3(2x−3). Thus 5x+5=6x−9, so x=14.", "topic": "Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities", "note": "Original PDF visually checked; isolated complete question and independently verified the solution.", "source_document": "Math_Question_Bank_Ghoneem_Clean.pdf", "source_page": 121, "extra_sources": []}, {"id": "clean:HOA 207", "old_status": "ocr_screened_not_verified", "status": "ready", "question_id": "d716144b-00bd-5c72-a0f9-53b52022b139", "stem": "Which equation is satisfied by x=1/8 and y=4?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "y=2/x"}, {"key": "B", "text": "y=2x"}, {"key": "C", "text": "y=1/(2x)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "x=2y"}], "type": "mcq", "correct": "C", "explanation": "For x=1/8, 1/(2x)=1/(1/4)=4, matching y. The other equations give respectively y=16, y=1/4, or x=8.", "topic": "Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities", "note": "Reworded as checking the given pair; one point cannot uniquely determine an entire function.", "source_document": "Math_Question_Bank_Ghoneem_Clean.pdf", "source_page": 122, "extra_sources": []}]$payload$::jsonb) LOOP
